  "function_translated_nl": "Lesson context: lesson_05. Let triangle PQR be a triangle with sides PQ = 30. Let segment XY be a segment. Let segment PX be a segment with length 12. Let segment PY be a segment with length 9. Let point X be a point on PQ. Let point Y be a point on PR. Segments PX and XQ and PY and YR are proportional with ratio 0.6. By the triangle proportionality theorem, a line parallel to QR divides the other two sides proportionally into segments PX and PY. Find the length of XY.",
  "student_facing_prose": "# Triangle XY and the Proportional Segments\n\nYou're working with triangle PQR, where side PQ measures 30 units. Points X and Y lie on sides PQ and PR respectively, with PX = 12 and PY = 9.\n\nBecause X and Y divide the two sides of the triangle proportionally \u2014 the ratio of PX to XQ equals the ratio of PY to YR, both equal to 0.6 \u2014 the segment XY must be parallel to the third side QR by the triangle proportionality theorem.\n\nSince XY is parallel to QR, triangle PXY is a dilation of triangle PQR, centered at vertex P. This means the sides of the two triangles are proportional with the same scale factor.\n\n**Find the length of segment XY.**"
}